WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.039
You press the Back button in your browser

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.307
and you land on the page you

3
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.510
were on a moment ago.

4
00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:08.610
Press it again

5
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.580
and you go one page further back.

6
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.380
In a text editor, you press Undo

7
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.480
and your most recent change disappears.

8
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.360
Press it again

9
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.090
and the change before that one disappears.

10
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.430
Both of these remember a history

11
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.130
and both give it back in the same order

12
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.360
the most recent thing first.

13
00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.286
What is the simplest data structure

14
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.390
that behaves exactly like that?

15
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.717
It is called a stack

16
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.385
and it is the first structure

17
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.450
in this chapter.

18
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.132
In this video we'll see

19
00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.910
what a stack promises, what it costs

20
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.600
and the two ways to build one.

21
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.112
A stack is a collection where you

22
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.090
can only work with one end.

23
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.690
You add an item on top.

24
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.030
You look at the top.

25
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.700
You remove the top.

26
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.900
Think of a pile of plates.

27
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.360
You put a plate on top

28
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.820
and when you want one

29
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.500
you take it from the top.

30
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.730
Nobody pulls a plate out of the middle.

31
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.934
The rule has a name: last in, first out.

32
00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.107
The last item that went in is the

33
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.050
first item that comes out.

34
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.880
People often write it L I F O.

35
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.510
A stack supports a handful of operations.

36
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.950
Push adds an item on top.

37
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.735
Pop removes the top item and returns it.

38
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.635
Top returns the top item

39
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.140
without removing it.

40
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.640
Two more questions are handy

41
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.080
how many items are there

42
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.250
and is the stack empty?

43
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.190
That is the whole interface.

44
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.498
Pop and top both need an item to work with

45
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.621
so asking for them on an empty

46
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.360
stack is an error.

47
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.830
Let's run a small example.

48
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.070
Start with an empty stack and push three

49
00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.500
then seven, then one.

50
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.560
Now the top is one.

51
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.920
If we pop, we get one back

52
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.410
and the stack is down to three and seven.

53
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.510
Pop again and we get seven.

54
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.270
Pop again, and we get three.

55
00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.690
We pushed three, seven, one

56
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.050
and we got them back as one, seven, three

57
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.440
exactly reversed.

58
00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.020
That reversal is not an accident.

59
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.078
It's the whole point of a stack

60
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.906
and it is why stacks are

61
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.550
good at undoing things.

62
00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.540
You may be thinking

63
00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.440
a list can do all of this and more.

64
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.170
Why restrict ourselves?

65
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.250
Because the restriction is a promise.

66
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.950
If you hand someone a stack

67
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.688
they can't peek at the middle or reorder it

68
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.621
so the structure never has to maintain

69
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.840
anything except the top.

70
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.780
A smaller promise is easier to keep

71
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.420
and as we'll see

72
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.469
it can be kept very cheaply.

73
00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:41.817
Choosing the right restriction

74
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.950
is a design skill in itself.

75
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.780
How much work does each operation take?

76
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:48.600
Push

77
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.360
pop and top only ever touch the top item.

78
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.570
They don't look at the other items at all.

79
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.878
So each takes a constant number of steps

80
00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.639
no matter whether the stack holds

81
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.930
ten items or ten million.

82
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.960
In Big-O terms, that's O of one.

83
00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.120
There's one footnote

84
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.160
for one of the two implementations

85
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.870
and we'll meet it in the code.

86
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.980
Stacks show up everywhere.

87
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.307
Undo and Back, as we saw.

88
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.594
Checking that the brackets

89
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.880
in a program match

90
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.770
which we'll do in video four.

91
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.670
And the one every program relies on

92
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.670
the call stack.

93
00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.970
Each function call pushes a frame

94
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.532
each return pops it.

95
00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.555
In Chapter four we'll see recursion

96
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.320
through exactly this lens.

97
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.590
There are two natural ways to build a stack.

98
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.070
Keep the items in an array

99
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.690
with the top at the end.

100
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.190
Or link the items together in a chain

101
00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.900
with the top at the front.

102
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.600
The array is compact and fast

103
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.160
but it has a fixed size

104
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.940
so it sometimes has to grow.

105
00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.980
The chain grows one item at a time

106
00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.450
but every item carries a pointer.

107
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.724
Both give the same behaviour.

108
00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.576
Which one is better is a

109
00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.850
question of trade-offs

110
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.600
and we'll look at both next.

111
00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.530
To sum up.

112
00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.870
A stack is last in, first out.

113
00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.570
You can push, pop, and look at the top

114
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.820
and nothing else.

115
00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.748
Every operation takes constant time

116
00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.093
and the restriction is what

117
00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.330
makes that possible.

118
00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.950
In the next part

119
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.000
we write both versions in code and run them.

120
00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:24.187
Now let's build both stacks in Java.

121
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.977
Here is the array-based stack.

122
00:04:26.977 --> 00:04:30.760
It is generic, so ArrayStack of E works

123
00:04:30.857 --> 00:04:32.251
for any element type.

124
00:04:32.317 --> 00:04:34.267
We keep an array called data

125
00:04:34.337 --> 00:04:36.484
and an int called size.

126
00:04:36.577 --> 00:04:38.442
The top item always lives

127
00:04:38.517 --> 00:04:41.247
at index size minus one.

128
00:04:41.597 --> 00:04:43.786
Java does not let us create an array

129
00:04:43.847 --> 00:04:45.607
of a generic type directly

130
00:04:45.607 --> 00:04:48.987
so we create an array of Object and cast it.

131
00:04:48.987 --> 00:04:51.359
That is the one ugly line

132
00:04:51.447 --> 00:04:53.009
and the compiler warning is

133
00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:54.237
safe to suppress here.

134
00:04:54.587 --> 00:04:56.984
Push stores the new item at index

135
00:04:57.057 --> 00:04:59.737
size and increments size.

136
00:04:59.737 --> 00:05:02.677
Pop decrements size, reads the item

137
00:05:02.677 --> 00:05:04.260
and sets that slot to null

138
00:05:04.317 --> 00:05:05.953
so the array doesn't keep a reference

139
00:05:05.997 --> 00:05:08.194
to an object the stack has dropped.

140
00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:11.899
Both pop and top throw a NoSuchElementException

141
00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:13.427
when the stack is empty.

142
00:05:14.007 --> 00:05:15.897
When the array is full

143
00:05:15.897 --> 00:05:19.217
push calls resize with twice the length

144
00:05:19.217 --> 00:05:21.017
it allocates a new array

145
00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:23.057
copies every item across

146
00:05:23.057 --> 00:05:25.327
and replaces the old one.

147
00:05:25.677 --> 00:05:27.287
We start with a capacity of

148
00:05:27.347 --> 00:05:29.593
two and push five items.

149
00:05:29.687 --> 00:05:31.736
The capacity stays at two for

150
00:05:31.807 --> 00:05:32.967
the first two pushes

151
00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:36.827
doubles to four at the third, stays at four

152
00:05:36.827 --> 00:05:38.837
and doubles to eight at the fifth.

153
00:05:39.187 --> 00:05:42.317
Copying takes time proportional to the size

154
00:05:42.317 --> 00:05:43.757
but it happens rarely.

155
00:05:43.757 --> 00:05:45.217
Because we double

156
00:05:45.217 --> 00:05:47.589
most pushes cost one step.

157
00:05:47.677 --> 00:05:50.231
The amortized analysis video in Chapter

158
00:05:50.297 --> 00:05:52.691
one showed that the average cost per

159
00:05:52.757 --> 00:05:54.827
push is still constant.

160
00:05:55.407 --> 00:05:58.119
The linked stack needs no capacity.

161
00:05:58.197 --> 00:06:01.321
Each item lives in a small private static

162
00:06:01.397 --> 00:06:04.180
Node class holding the element and a

163
00:06:04.257 --> 00:06:05.684
reference to the next node.

164
00:06:05.737 --> 00:06:07.666
The stack keeps just a head

165
00:06:07.737 --> 00:06:09.487
reference and a size.

166
00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:11.712
Push creates a node whose

167
00:06:11.787 --> 00:06:13.467
next is the old head

168
00:06:13.467 --> 00:06:15.878
then makes that node the new head.

169
00:06:15.947 --> 00:06:17.983
Pop reads the head's element and

170
00:06:18.047 --> 00:06:19.837
moves head to the next node.

171
00:06:20.187 --> 00:06:21.689
No copying, ever

172
00:06:21.777 --> 00:06:23.652
and every call takes constant

173
00:06:23.717 --> 00:06:26.777
time in the worst case, not only on average.

174
00:06:26.777 --> 00:06:29.357
The price is one extra reference

175
00:06:29.357 --> 00:06:32.547
and one extra object, per item.

176
00:06:33.127 --> 00:06:34.817
In the standard library

177
00:06:34.817 --> 00:06:37.617
Java has an old class called Stack.

178
00:06:37.617 --> 00:06:38.877
Avoid it.

179
00:06:38.877 --> 00:06:40.517
It extends Vector

180
00:06:40.517 --> 00:06:42.557
so every method is synchronized

181
00:06:42.557 --> 00:06:44.673
which costs time you don't need

182
00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.379
and it exposes index-based methods

183
00:06:47.457 --> 00:06:49.031
that let you reach into the middle

184
00:06:49.077 --> 00:06:50.967
and break the stack promise.

185
00:06:51.317 --> 00:06:53.863
The Java documentation itself recommends

186
00:06:53.927 --> 00:06:56.267
the Deque interface instead.

187
00:06:56.267 --> 00:06:58.767
ArrayDeque has push, pop and peek

188
00:06:58.767 --> 00:07:01.077
and it is the stack you should use.

189
00:07:01.657 --> 00:07:03.955
So an array stack is fast

190
00:07:04.047 --> 00:07:05.746
but sometimes copies

191
00:07:05.827 --> 00:07:08.744
and a linked stack never copies but

192
00:07:08.827 --> 00:07:10.727
carries an object per item.

193
00:07:10.727 --> 00:07:12.447
Both give you push

194
00:07:12.447 --> 00:07:14.657
pop and top in constant time.

195
00:07:15.007 --> 00:07:18.548
Next up: queues, where the first item in

196
00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:20.607
is the first one out.

